Check this out for learning to touch soft objects, with medical applications in mind. The system learns to visualize the inner structure of soft objects purely through touch. The paper titled "More with LESS: Local Scene Representation for Tactile Imaging" has been accepted to RSS 2026.

It leverages the locality of touch for large-scale tactile self-supervised learning. The video demonstrates how the system visualizes soft objects through tactile sensing.

People sometimes ask why fine-tune when general-purpose models keep getting better. Bridgewater's work is a good reminder that with the right data—here, expert judgments—you can beat prompting-only approaches by a lot.

@ddkang and the Bridgewater AIA Labs team are great—glad to see them sharing this.

@tinkerapi: "Sorting which financial docs are worth an analyst's time is surprisingly hard for frontier LLMs. With an expert-labeled dataset and on-policy distillation, Bridgewater fine-tuned a model to do it reliably and cheaply."

The linked paper explains that using expert-labeled data and fine-tuning on Tinker, a custom model outperforms large general models on financial information filtering at a fraction of the cost.
